 through characters 'e', 'r', 's', and 'i' with their respective modular arithmetic operations to track the running hash value. Continuing with step 95 for the period character, then converting the final hash value to hexadecimal representation. I'll stick with lowercase hex for consistency. Now I'm building out a complete HTML page with a header, hero section featuring that hash class, product showcase, about section, and footer, all styled with inline CSS.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The fetch is returning plain text instead of HTML, so I'll need to construct a full page structure myself with a header, navigation, hero section, featured products, about section, and footer. I'm computing a hash using DJB2 on the concatenated What and Why fields to track this improvement, which adds a hero section to the YogaDirect page. I'm going to compute the DJB2 hash by processing the concatenated string character by character, keeping the value modulo 2^32 to avoid overflow. I've shortened both the \"What\" and \"Why\" descriptions to make the calculation more manageable—around 94 characters total instead of 240—and I'm carefully counting through each character to apply the hash formula iteratively. Computing the hash values... Continuing through the remaining characters: 'e' gives 2059995172 after the modulo operation, then 'r' yields 3555331350, and I'm working through 'o' which multiplies to get 117325934550 before applying the modulo. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: 'c', 't', and starting 'i', applying the same modular arithmetic pattern with multiplications by 33 and character code additions. Continuing the hash computation through steps 20-22, processing 'w', 'i', and 't' with their ASCII values and applying the modulo operations at each stage. Continuing through the hash computation steps, working through characters 'a', 'd', and 'l' with their respective modular arithmetic operations to track the rolling 