ular arithmetic to track the running hash value as each character gets processed. The hex conversion checks out to 2A09F6B8, so Hash3 is complete. Now I'm processing the fourth string \"Added Shop All linkGuides shoppers to full catalog\" and working through the hash computation starting with the \"Added \" prefix. Continuing the hash computation through characters 'A', 'l', 'l', and space, applying the modular arithmetic operations and accumulating the results. Continuing through characters u, i, d, and e, applying the same modular arithmetic pattern with multiplications by 33, subtracting powers of 2, and adding character codes to track the running hash value. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters in \"shopper\", working through the modular arithmetic for each step. Continuing through the hash computation for characters 't', 'o', space, 'f', and 'u', tracking the modular arithmetic operations and character code offsets at each step. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters in \"catalog\", working through the modular arithmetic for each step. I've verified the hex conversion is correct at 0xF4608F7E, which gives me the hash identifier HDLmClassF4608F7E. Now I'm building out the HTML page structure with a header navigation, hero section, product grid featuring yoga accessories like mats and blocks, an about section, and footer, using placeholder images for the products.\n\nFor the product images, I'll use inline SVG data URIs styled as product illustrations rather than guessing at real URLs from yogadirect, since I don't have access to their actual image paths. I'm setting up the product listings with their prices, ratings, and availability status like \"Only 12 left\" and \"Ships today\" to match what I'd expect from the original page.\n\nI'm also noting the remaining products in the catalog—the Cotton Yoga Strap, Supportive Yoga Bolster, Studio Bundle, and Cork Yoga Wheel—each with their discounted pricing