t paragraph, and a new testimonials section. I'll add star ratings to the testimonials from the studio owner, retail buyer, and event organizer, marking that section with the hash class. I'm keeping the page structure simple and faithful to the available content, using inline styles for the star ratings.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm going to retrieve that URL.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""},{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm constructing a complete HTML page for the YogaDirect homepage that incorporates the fetched text content along with a new testimonial section featuring star ratings from different customer types—a studio owner, retail buyer, and event organizer. I'll compute the DJB2 hash for this single improvement.\n\nI'm shortening the strings to make the hash calculation manageable: \"Added customer testimonials with star ratings.\" for the What, and \"Social proof builds trust and lifts conversions.\" for the Why. Now I'm computing the DJB2 hash by iterating through each character of the concatenated string, applying the formula h = (h * 33 + char_code) mod 2^32 step by step. Computing the hash value step by step using the DJB2 algorithm, multiplying by 33 and adding each character's ASCII value as I work through the string. Continuing through characters 'c', 'u', and 's', applying the same modular arithmetic operations with each character's ASCII value, tracking the hash state as it evolves through each step. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through the modular arithmetic for 'e', 'r', space, and 't'. Continuing through characters 'i', 'm', 'o', and starting 'n', computing the rolling hash by multiplying by 33, adding each character's ASCII value, and taking modulo operations to keep the values manageable. Continuing through the hash computation for each character in \"details\": processing 's', the space character, 'w', and 'i', applying the multiplicat