ed. Continuing the hash computation through characters e, r, space, c, l, and i... Continuing the hash computation through characters i, f, t, s, and another s, applying the same multiply-by-33-and-add pattern with modular arithmetic adjustments. Converting to hexadecimal by extracting each nibble digit from the decimal value, starting with the highest order bits and working down through the remainder. Verifying the hex conversion by reconstructing the original value from each digit position, confirming it equals 3909235534. Now I'm moving on to hash generation and examining the next improvement about star ratings on product cards as a social proof element. Continuing through the hash computation for \"Start Social proof drives clicks\", working through each character's contribution with modular arithmetic to keep the values manageable. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through the modular arithmetic to track the running value as each letter gets processed. Continuing through the hash computation iterations, working through characters like 'l', space, 'p', 'r', and 'o' with their respective modular arithmetic operations to track the running hash value. Continuing the hash computation through characters v, e, s, c, and l, tracking the modular arithmetic operations and differences at each step. Converting the hash to hexadecimal by dividing by powers of 256 and extracting each byte, then verifying the result matches the original value.\n\nNow I'm working through improvement 3 about sale and savings badges, converting the text string into its character codes to build the hash input. Continuing the hash calculation through characters 6-10, tracking the modulo operations as the values grow larger with each multiplication and addition step. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters in \"spacing badge\", tracking the modular arithmetic and differences at each step. Continuing through the hash computation with characters 'u', 'n', 't', 's', and the space character, applying the same multiply-by-33-and-add pattern with modular arithmetic adjustments at each step. Continuing the hash computation... Converting to hex and verifying the result checks out to 189ea62b, giving me Hash3 = HDLmClass189ea62b. Now moving on to the next improvement. Verifying the character counts for both parts and then computing the hash value using the standard algorithm, multiplying by 33 and adding each character's ASCII value sequentially. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through modular arithmetic with the rolling hash values and applying the appropriate modulo operations at each step. Continuing the hash computation through characters n, space, c, a, and r, tracking the modular arithmetic operations and differences at each step. Continuing the hash computation through characters v, e, s, space, and c... Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through the modular arithmetic to extract each hex digit of the final hash value. Now I'm verifying the hex conversion is correct, then moving on to build the full HTML page with a header, hero section, product grid featuring typical yoga equipment like mats and blocks, an about section, and footer—all styled inline.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The fetch only returned plain text instead of HTML, so I don't have the actual page structure. I'll need to construct a proper HTML page for the YogaDirect homepage with improved product cards based on what I know, though ideally I'd have the original markup to work from. Let me try a different approach to get the full HTML content.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""},{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm reconstructing the homepage with enhanced product cards that include star ratings, review counts, pricing with savings badges, quick-add buttons, trust indicators, and stock urge