ular arithmetic and accumulating values as I process 'e' and 'r'. Converting to hex by dividing through successive powers of 16, working down from 0x100000 through 0x10 to extract each digit, giving me 0x721E70D1.\n\nNow I'm building out the HTML for the YogaDirect homepage, reconstructing a complete page from the text extract with header navigation, search functionality, featured products, about section, and footer — including a sticky search bar at the top.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The fetch is returning plain text instead of HTML, so I need to try a different approach—maybe requesting a variant URL that will give me the actual HTML structure I need.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""},{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm building out the HTML structure for the yogadirect.com homepage with the original content—header, navigation, search functionality, featured products, about section, and footer—while implementing the sticky search bar as the key improvement.\n\nNow I need to compute the DJB2 hash of the concatenated What and Why values to document this change. I'm shortening the strings to make the calculation more manageable: \"Sticky search bar\" combined with a concise explanation of why persistent search access improves the shopping experience and conversion rates. Computing the DJB2 hash step by step... Continuing through the remaining characters, computing the hash value modulo 2^32 after each multiplication and character addition for 'e', 'a', 'r', and starting on 'c'. Continuing through characters 14-16, computing the hash values modulo 2^32 for each step, tracking the intermediate results as I process 'b' and 'a'. Continuing the hash calculations for the remaining characters in the search phrase, working through the modular arithmetic to compute each successive hash value. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through the modular arithmetic for 's', 't', and 'a'. 