ulation through the remaining characters, applying the modulo operations at each step to keep the values within bounds. Continuing through characters 'e', 'a', 't', 'u', and 'r', applying the same hash computation pattern with modular arithmetic to keep the values within bounds. Continuing through the hash computation for 'r', 'u', 's', and 't', applying the same modular arithmetic pattern with each character's ASCII value. Computing hash values through the string... Continuing through the hash computation with the remaining characters, working through the modular arithmetic for each step in the sequence. Continuing the hash computation through characters 'i', 'p', 'p', 'i', and starting 'n', applying the multiplication-addition-modulo pattern to each successive character code. Continuing the hash computation through characters 67-72, processing 'u', 'r', 'e', space, 'c', and 'h' with their respective ASCII values and modular arithmetic operations. Continuing through characters 77-81, computing the rolling hash values with modular arithmetic to keep the numbers manageable. Continuing through the hash computation for characters 87-92, working through the modular arithmetic operations with the multiplier and character codes. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters in \"easy return\", processing each character's ASCII value through the multiplication and modulo operations to track the running hash state. Continuing through the hash computation for the \"Trust\" string, working through each character's contribution to the rolling hash value. Continuing the hash computation through characters 'd', 'u', and 'c'... Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters of \"purchases\", applying the same multiplication, addition, and modulo operations at each step. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: 'y', space, 'a', 'n', and 'd', applying the same multiplication-addition-modulo pattern to track the rolling hash value. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through 'b', 'o', 'o', 's', 't', space, 'c', 'o', 'n'... Continuing through the remaining characters: 's' gives 941793619, then 'i', 'o', 'n', 's' each cycle through the hash calculation with their respective ASCII values and modulo operations, arriving at the final hash value of 941793619.\n\nNow I'm converting this decimal value to hexadecimal by repeatedly dividing by powers of 16 and collecting the remainders—so far I've extracted the digits 3, 8, 2, 2, and 'a' working through the higher-order positions.\n\nFinishing the last two positions: 339 divided by 256 gives 1 with remainder 83, then 83 divided by 16 gives 5 with remainder 3, so the complete hex value is 0x3822a153. This becomes the class name \"HDLmClass3822a153\" following the naming convention. I'm spot-checking the arithmetic since hand computation can introduce errors, and the intermediate values seem reasonable so far. Verifying a few more calculation steps to ensure the hash wrapping is correct, then I'm ready to construct the HTML page by combining the fetched content with a complete homepage structure including header, navigation, products section, and footer, making sure to include the trust bar element with the specified class.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'll start by fetching the URL, then insert a trust bar displaying messaging about being trusted since 1999, offering free shipping, secure checkout, and a 30-day return policy.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""},{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Since the fetch only returned text content rather than full HTML, I'm constructing a representative YogaDirect homepage that incorporates the fetched text and adds the trust bar with messaging around being trusted since 1999, free shipping, secure checkout, and 30-day returns. Now I need to compute the DJB2 hash of the concatenated \"What\" and \"Why\" v