ing, working through each character's contribution to the rolling hash value. Continuing the hash computation through characters 'd', 'u', and 'c'... Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters of \"purchases\", applying the same multiplication, addition, and modulo operations at each step. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: 'y', space, 'a', 'n', and 'd', applying the same multiplication-addition-modulo pattern to track the rolling hash value. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through 'b', 'o', 'o', 's', 't', space, 'c', 'o', 'n'... Continuing through the remaining characters: 's' gives 941793619, then 'i', 'o', 'n', 's' each cycle through the hash calculation with their respective ASCII values and modulo operations, arriving at the final hash value of 941793619.\n\nNow I'm converting this decimal value to hexadecimal by repeatedly dividing by powers of 16 and collecting the remainders—so far I've extracted the digits 3, 8, 2, 2, and 'a' working through the higher-order positions.\n\nFinishing the last two positions: 339 divided by 256 gives 1 with remainder 83, then 83 divided by 16 gives 5 with remainder 3, so the complete hex value is 0x3822a153. This becomes the class name \"HDLmClass3822a153\" following the naming convention. I'm spot-checking the arithmetic since hand computation can introduce errors, and the intermediate values seem reasonable so far. Verifying a few more calculation steps to ensure the hash wrapping is correct, then I'm ready to construct the HTML page by combining the fetched content with a complete homepage structure including header, navigation, products section, and footer, making sure to include the trust bar element with the specified class.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":1,"signature":""}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":4073,"completion_tokens":15160,"total_tokens":19233,"cost":0.79873,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{