dded hero CTA banner\" paired with \"Drives visitors to shop now\"—then computing the DJB2 hash iteratively across the combined string using modular arithmetic. I'm reconsidering the string lengths—going with medium-length descriptions around 30-40 characters total per improvement to balance informativeness with computational feasibility. I'll pair concise \"What\" statements with brief \"Why\" explanations, like \"Add hero banner CTA\" with \"Boosts conversions\" for the first improvement.\n\nNow I'm mapping out ASCII values to compute the hash across all five improvements, starting with the character codes for each letter and symbol in the strings. Continuing with step 5, I'm multiplying by 33 and adding the next character code for 'h', then taking the modulo to keep it within the 32-bit range. Steps 6 and 7 follow the same pattern for 'e' and 'r', each time reducing the result back to the valid hash space. Continuing through steps 12-14, working through the modular arithmetic calculations with each character code, tracking the remainders as I apply the operations and subtract multiples of 2^32. Continuing through iterations 19-22, applying the same modular arithmetic operations with successive character codes to reduce the values step by step. Continuing through the iterations, tracking how each character's ASCII value transforms through the modular arithmetic sequence, working through characters like 'c', 'u', 'n', 'd', 'o', 'e', and 'r' as the values cycle through the calculations. Converting the remainder to hexadecimal by repeatedly dividing by powers of 16 to extract each digit...\n\nVerifying the result by expanding 0x7877D9DD back to decimal and confirming it matches the original value.\n\nNow moving on to the next improvement about trust badges. Continuing through the hash calculations for each character, tracking the running values as I apply the multiplication and modulo operations at each step. Continuing through the hash computation steps, working through characters 16-20 with modular arithmetic to keep values within the 32-bit range. Continuing through the hash computation iterations, working through characters 'o', 'p', 'p', 'e' with their respective modular arithmetic operations to track the running hash value. Finishing the calculation for step 36, then converting the final result to hexadecimal by repeatedly dividing by powers of 16 to extract each digit, which gives me 8ED5A15C.\n\nNow I'm verifying this hex conversion by multiplying each digit back by its place value and summing them to confirm I get the original decimal number. The hash is HDLmClass8ED5A15C, and I'm moving on to the next improvement which involves character analysis for a product pricing feature. Continuing through the hash computation steps, working through characters like 't', space, 'p', 'r', 'i' with their respective modular arithmetic operations to track the running hash value. Continuing the hash computation through characters 20-25, processing each character by multiplying by 33, adding its ASCII value, and applying modular arithmetic with various divisors to keep the values bounded. Continuing through the hash computation for \"purch\" and into \"a\", tracking the modular arithmetic operations at each character step. The hex conversion checks out to 1239439516, so the hash for this entry is HDLmClass49E0589C. Now I'm moving into the fourth improvement with the text \"Add testimonialsAdds social proof\" and starting the hash computation from the beginning with an initial value of 2088921742. Continuing through characters 12-15, computing the rolling hash values with modular arithmetic at each step, tracking the character codes and intermediate results as the hash evolves. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: space, 's', 'o', 'c', 'i', and 'a', applying the same multiply-by-33, add-character-code, and modulo-subtraction pattern at each step. Continuing through the remaining characters, I'm processin