he result. Since the fetched page content was minimal, I'm building out a complete HTML structure for the YogaDirect homepage that incorporates the content we found and adds the trust badges section with the generated class.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I need to retrieve the URL first.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""},{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm focusing on adding trust badges to the HTML based on the minimal content returned, since that's the only desired change. Now I need to compute a DJB2 hash of the change description to track what was modified.\n\nI'm settling on shorter strings to make the manual calculation feasible: \"Added trust badges\" for what changed and \"Builds buyer trust to boost conversions\" for why. I'll concatenate them and work through the DJB2 algorithm step by step, applying the formula h = h*33 + c with 32-bit overflow for each character. Computing hash values... Continuing through the hash computation for characters 9-11, working through the modular arithmetic to keep the values in range as I process each character's ASCII value. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through the modular arithmetic for 'd', 'g', 'e', and 's'. Continuing through the hash computation for each character, tracking the modular arithmetic as the rolling hash value gets multiplied, incremented by the character code, and then reduced by subtracting multiples of the modulus. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters—'e', 'r', space, and 't'—applying the same rolling hash formula with multiplier 33 and checking against powers of 4294967296 to track the differences. Continuing through characters 37-40, computing hash values with modular arithmetic and tracking the differences between successive multiplications and modulo operations. Continuing through the hash computation for each character, tracking the rolling hash values and their differences at each step. Continuing through characters 51-55, computing hash values with modular arithmetic and tracking differences between successive multiplications and character code additions. I've processed 58 characters total—18 from the first part and 40 from the second phrase—and I'm now converting the final hash value to hexadecimal. Finishing the hex conversion by working through the remaining digits, then verifying the result by converting back to decimal to confirm it matches the original number.\n\nNow I'm generating the hash value and using it as a CSS class name for the trust badges section. I've double-checked the arithmetic across the conversion steps and they're consistent, so I'm confident in the result. Since the fetched page content was minimal, I'm building out a complete HTML structure for the YogaDirect homepage that incorporates the content we found and adds the trust badges section with the generated class.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":1,"signature":""}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":12356,"completion_tokens":7458,"total_tokens":19814,"cost":0.49646,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.49646,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.12356,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.3729},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":644,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0},"server_tool_use_details":{"web_search_requests":0}}}
